<article class="env-lemma" id="0624">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0624">Lemma <span data-tag="0624">9.1.8.4</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be a regular cardinal and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a simplicial set which is a $\kappa $-filtered $\infty $-category which satisfies condition $(\ast )$ of Lemma <a data-tag="02QB" href="/tag/02QB">9.1.8.2</a>. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the colimit of a diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  A \rightarrow \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}\quad \quad (\alpha \in A) \mapsto X_{\alpha }, \]
</div>
<p> where $(A, \leq )$ is a $\kappa $-directed partially ordered set and each $X_{\alpha }$ is a simplicial set of the form $K_{\alpha }^{\triangleright }$ for some $\kappa $-small simplicial set $K_{\alpha }$. Moreover, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\lambda $-small for some regular cardinal $\lambda $ with $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $, then we can arrange that $A$ is also $\lambda $-small. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix a regular cardinal $\lambda $ such that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\lambda $-small. If $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $, then we can choose a $\lambda $-small collection $\{  K_{\alpha } \} _{\alpha \in A}$ of $\kappa $-small simplicial subsets of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, such that every $\kappa $-small simplicial subset of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is contained in some $K_{\alpha }$ (Lemma <a data-tag="061Y" href="/tag/061Y">9.1.7.18</a>). Since $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfies condition $(\ast )$, each $K_{\alpha }$ is contained in a larger simplicial subset $X_{\alpha } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which is isomorphic to the cone $K_{\alpha }^{\triangleright }$. We regard $A$ as a partially ordered set, where $\alpha \leq \beta $ if $X_{\alpha }$ is contained in $X_{\beta }$. Then $(A, \leq )$ is automatically $\kappa $-directed (Remark <a data-tag="061Z" href="/tag/061Z">9.1.7.19</a>), and the colimit $\varinjlim _{\alpha \in A} X_{\alpha }$ identifies with the union $\bigcup _{\alpha } X_{\alpha } = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
